<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Verdun</h1><p class="st-meta">997 CE – 1648 CE · capital Verdun</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the Three Bishoprics of Lorraine, a church principality on the Meuse that passed from the Empire to France between 1552 and 1648.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, where the Carolingian empire was divided in 843, was an ancient bishopric. In 997 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto III</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Haimo the comital rights over the city and its district, making the bishops princes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' temporal lands were small and lay along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, and they faced constant pressure from the counts of Bar and the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a>. The citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> gained wide self-government and imperial-city status, limiting the bishops' power at home. The city was a trading centre, known for its sugared almonds.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> in 1552, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 confirmed French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a>. Verdun became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a> and later a major fortress.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-verdun.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Verdun" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: OwenBlacker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trois-%C3%89v%C3%AAch%C3%A9s.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>997 CE – 1648 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Verdun</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Haimo of Verdun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bishopric of Verdun, Hochstift Verdun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1322&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-verdun" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Verdun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4804 463 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Verdun lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5124 719L5229 694M5124 719L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5124 719h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5124 719h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Verdun. <a href="/atlas?year=1322&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-verdun">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

